# 19. BACKUP AND RESTORE

Backup and restore are essential features.

Create a dedicated Backup and Restore section inside Settings.

## 19.1 Full Backup

A complete backup must include:

- Entire database.
- All folders.
- All subfolders.
- All documents.
- Full rich-text content.
- Document formatting.
- Images.
- Audio files.
- Video files.
- Other attachments.
- Document-media relationships.
- Required settings and metadata.
- Cross-platform identifiers.

The backup must include actual media file contents, not only database paths.

## 19.2 Backup Options

Provide:

- Manual backup.
- Automatic periodic backup.
- User-selected backup destination.
- Backup to shared device storage.
- Backup to a user-selected Documents folder.
- Backup to supported external storage.
- Backup to USB storage where Android supports it.
- Backup to an accessible cloud storage provider through the Android file picker.
- Backup file size display.
- Last backup date.
- Backup success or failure status.
- Backup integrity verification.

Automatic backup should be optional and configured by the user.

Do not silently overwrite the only available valid backup.

## 19.3 Critical Android Uninstallation Requirement

Android normally deletes private application data when an application is uninstalled.

Therefore, never rely exclusively on the application's private internal storage to preserve information.

Implement an external backup system that stores complete backup files outside the private application directory.

Use the Android Storage Access Framework so the user can select a persistent backup location.

Make it clear that:

- Active database and media files are stored inside application-managed storage.
- A separate backup copy must be stored outside the private application directory.
- The application cannot guarantee recovery after uninstall if no external backup exists.
- After reinstalling, the user may need to select the previous backup location again.

Provide a clear setup option encouraging the user to select an external backup destination.

Show the date and status of the latest successful backup.

Warn the user when the backup is outdated or has not been created.

## 19.4 Restore After Reinstallation

After the application is reinstalled:

1. Detect whether an external backup can be accessed.
2. Offer a Restore Data option.
3. Let the user select an existing backup file.
4. Verify backup integrity.
5. Validate backup schema version.
6. Restore the database.
7. Restore all folders and subfolders.
8. Restore all documents and rich formatting.
9. Restore all images, audio files, videos, and attachments.
10. Rebuild document-media relationships.
11. Verify restored content.
12. Report successful or failed recovery.

Restoration must preserve the original organization and content.

Never claim successful restoration unless the restored information has been verified.

Create a recovery point before destructive restore operations.

